      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Walking Pads</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines or measured their noise. They were chosen from
            published specifications, maker and retailer listings, warranty terms and independent
            hands-on coverage, checked on 15 October 2026, and the claims on this page are limited
            to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            Because there is no common noise test for walking pads, we did not rank machines by
            their decibel claims. Instead we looked for things that give a physical reason to expect
            less noise, or independent reporting that a machine was quiet: a heavier frame and a
            stiffer deck, which pass less footfall energy into the floor; a walking-only speed range;
            features that cut the incidental noise people complain about, such as beeps; and
            independent reviewers who used the machine in a shared office and commented on noise.
            Each machine also had to be on sale under its current name, and its basic figures had to
            agree between the maker and at least one independent source.
          </p>
          <p>
            Several pads are missing on purpose. <strong>Sperax</strong> walking pads are excluded
            entirely: in April 2026 the US Consumer Product Safety Commission warned consumers to stop
            using them. We searched the CPSC recall database for every other brand on this page —
            WalkingPad and its maker KingSmith, LifeSpan, iMovR and InMovement — and for the brands
            we considered and left out, and found no recalls. The <strong>DeerRun Z10</strong> was
            described by one independent reviewer as quiet, but the same reviewer found its beeps
            cannot be silenced, and DeerRun sells several Z10 variants and model years under similar
            names. UREVO's <strong>CyberPad for Office</strong> is advertised at 35 dB on the
            strength of UREVO's own laboratory testing, and we found no independent coverage of it.
            Two pads that an independent reviewer singled out as particularly quiet — a UREVO pad
            and a Maksone wooden pad — no longer had live listings at the addresses that reviewer
            linked to. And we left out LifeSpan's TX6-GlowUp and TR5000B Pro+ because we could not
            find independent coverage of the current versions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-downstairs-hears" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Downstairs Actually Hears</h2>
<p>
            Walking-pad noise comes in two kinds, and they travel very differently. Most buying
            decisions focus on the wrong one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Airborne noise: the hum in your room</h3>
          <p>
            The motor, the belt running over the deck and the rollers make sound that travels
            through the air. It is what you hear standing next to the pad, what a colleague on a
            video call might hear, and what a decibel meter beside the machine measures. A floor and
            ceiling between two flats block a good deal of airborne sound, so for the neighbour
            below, motor hum is rarely the complaint. It matters more for people in the same room or
            on the other side of a thin internal wall.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Impact noise: the thud through the floor</h3>
          <p>
            Every step lands on the belt, the belt presses on the deck, the deck pushes on the
            frame and the frame pushes on your floor. That impact goes into the building structure
            itself — joists, slab, ceiling — and structures carry the low, rhythmic thump of
            footsteps very efficiently. This is structure-borne noise, and it is what people below a
            walking pad usually complain about: not a whine, but a steady thud-thud at walking pace,
            sometimes with a rattle from whatever the floor sets vibrating.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical consequence is that a pad with a very quiet motor can still be a bad
            neighbour, and a pad with an audible hum can be a good one, if it is heavier and better
            damped. A light pad sitting directly on a suspended timber floor is close to the worst
            case. Our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for apartments</a>
            covers the same problem for full-size machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            A simple test settles which kind you have. Ask someone to stand in the room below while
            you walk at your usual pace. If they hear a hum or a whine, the problem is airborne and
            the machine is the issue. If they hear a rhythmic thud, it is impact, and placement,
            mass and damping are the fixes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decibel-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How to Read a Decibel Claim</h2>
<p>
            Nearly every walking pad listing quotes a noise figure, and nearly all of them are
            impossible to compare. Two examples from the same brand on the day we checked make the
            point: one retailer's listing claims the WalkingPad Z1 runs below 40 dB, while
            WalkingPad's own page for the A1 Pro claims under 65 dB. Taken at face value, that is an
            enormous difference between two similar pads. More likely, the two figures were measured
            in different ways, or not measured in any comparable way at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            A decibel figure means little without four pieces of information that listings almost
            never give:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Distance.</strong> Sound level drops as you move away from the source. A figure taken a few feet from the machine is not the same as one taken at the motor cover.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed.</strong> Motor and belt noise rise with speed. A figure at the slowest setting says little about a brisk walk.</li>
            <li><strong>Load.</strong> A pad running with nobody on it is quieter than one carrying a walker. Many quoted figures appear to be unloaded.</li>
            <li><strong>Weighting and background.</strong> Meters can weight frequencies differently, and a quiet room changes the result.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            And even a carefully measured airborne figure tells you nothing about impact noise in the
            room below, which is the complaint that matters in most flats. So treat a listing's dB
            claim as the seller's description of the motor, read independent reviews that describe
            noise in plain words — "fairly quiet at desk speeds", "beeps cannot be muted" — and
            weigh mass, deck and placement more heavily than any number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-makes-quiet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Makes One Walking Pad Quieter Than Another</h2>
<p>
            Without a common test, you are left reasoning from construction. These are the
            differences that plausibly matter, roughly in order of how much.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mass and stiffness.</strong> A heavier frame and a stiffer deck move less with
            each step, so less of each footfall becomes floor vibration. This is the main reason the
            two non-folding office treadmills on this page, both considerably heavier than the
            roughly 50 to 60 lb folding pads, are our choices for flats. It is also why folding pads, which
            have a hinge in the middle of the deck, are not the first choice when the people below
            are the concern.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speed.</strong> Walking at 2 mph is much gentler on the floor than walking at 4,
            and far gentler than jogging. Machines capped at walking speeds — the Unsit stops at 2
            mph — remove the temptation. If you live above someone, keep the pace that you can
            maintain with a soft, rolling step.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cushioning.</strong> Shock absorbers and soft belt layers soften the landing
            for the walker, and some of that softening reduces the impact reaching the frame. Makers
            describe these systems in marketing terms that cannot be compared, so treat them as a
            tie-breaker rather than a reason to buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incidental noise.</strong> In practice, many complaints are about beeps on
            every speed change, a remote that chirps, a hinge that clunks when the pad is unfolded,
            or a belt that squeaks for want of lubricant. An independent reviewer noted that the A1
            Pro can make a loud tearing sound when unfolded if the belt sticks, and that the DeerRun
            Z10's beeps cannot be silenced. These are small things that become big things at 7 am.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Condition.</strong> A dry belt, a loose bolt or a belt that has drifted off centre
            makes noise that no specification predicts. Our guides to
            <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricating a walking pad</a>
            and to
            <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squeaking treadmill noise</a>
            cover the fixes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-or-fixed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Folding Pad or Office Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            The biggest decision on this page is not between brands but between two kinds of
            machine, and it is mostly decided by your room.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A folding pad</strong> — the three WalkingPad models here — weighs around 50 to
            60 lb, slides under a sofa or bed, and costs a few hundred dollars. It is the right
            choice when the pad cannot stay out, when you walk for an hour or so at a time, and when
            there is nobody below you or the floor is a concrete slab. Its weaknesses for a quiet
            flat are the same features that make it convenient: little mass, a thin deck and a hinge
            in the middle.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An office treadmill</strong> — the LifeSpan and the Unsit — is heavier, does
            not fold, and is designed to sit under a standing desk for hours a day. It costs several
            times as much. In return it has more mass and a stiffer deck to absorb each step, a belt
            built for long daily use, and a longer frame warranty. If you live above someone and
            plan to walk through much of the working day, it is the better neighbour.
          </p>
          <p>
            A middle path is a folding pad that, in practice, never moves: left in place on a dense
            mat with isolation feet underneath. That recovers some of what an office treadmill does
            for the floor, at a fraction of the price, though it will not match the mass. Our
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs treadmill comparison</a>
            covers the wider trade-offs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Setting Up a Walking Pad for the Neighbours</h2>
<p>
            How you set a pad up can matter more than which pad you buy. In rough order of
            usefulness:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Put something dense underneath.</strong> A dense rubber mat spreads the load and absorbs part of each impact; a thin vinyl mat mainly protects the floor. Our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mat guide</a> explains the difference, and our guide to <a href="/best-treadmill-anti-vibration-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">anti-vibration pads</a> covers the isolation feet that go under a machine. Avoid soft foam, which lets the pad rock.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep it off shared walls.</strong> A pad pushed against a party wall or a radiator pipe can pass vibration straight into the structure. Leave a gap.</li>
            <li><strong>Choose the spot.</strong> On a timber floor, a position near a load-bearing wall usually flexes less than the middle of a span. Over a neighbour's bedroom is the worst place.</li>
            <li><strong>Walk softly.</strong> Cushioned shoes and a shorter, rolling step reduce the impact far more than you might expect. A brisk walk with a heavy heel strike is the noisiest way to use any pad.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind the clock.</strong> Impact noise that is unnoticeable mid-afternoon can be intrusive early in the morning or late at night. If you can, walk when the flat below is likely to be empty or awake.</li>
            <li><strong>Maintain it.</strong> Lubricate the belt on the maker's schedule, check that it runs centred and tighten the frame bolts occasionally. A dry or loose pad gets noisier with age.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If you are buying a pad specifically to use under a standing desk, our guide to the
            <a href="/standing-desk-height-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standing desk height you need for a walking pad</a>
            covers the ergonomics, and the
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmills guide</a>
            covers the wider choice of machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apartment-checks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Before You Buy for an Apartment</h2>
<p>
            A few checks before ordering save an awkward conversation later.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Read the lease or building rules.</strong> Some buildings restrict exercise
            equipment or set quiet hours. It is better to know before the pad arrives.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Know your floor.</strong> A concrete slab carries impact noise less than a
            timber floor on joists, and a ground-floor flat has nobody below at all. If you are on an
            upper floor of a timber building, assume impact noise will travel and plan on a dense mat
            and a heavier machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the weight limit honestly.</strong> A pad used near its limit works its
            motor and deck harder, and in practice harder-worked pads tend to get noisier. Several
            pads on this page have limits around 220 to 242 lb, and our page on
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for heavy people</a>
            covers machines rated higher.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the return terms.</strong> You will only know how a pad sounds in your flat
            once it is there. A return window that covers a couple of weeks of real use, and a clear
            answer on who pays return shipping for a 50 to 160 lb box, is worth having.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plan for its life.</strong> Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-walking-pads-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long walking pads last</a>
            sets realistic expectations; noise that develops over months is often the first sign of
            wear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-week" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Testing a New Pad in Your Flat</h2>
<p>
            You only find out how a pad sounds in your building once it is in your building, so use
            the return window deliberately rather than hoping for the best.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Unbox and inspect before using.</strong> Keep the packaging until the return window closes; a 50 to 160 lb machine is hard to ship back without it.</li>
            <li><strong>Set it up properly first.</strong> Mat and feet in place, the pad level, the belt centred and lubricated as the manual says. Judging a pad on a bare floor straight out of the box tells you little.</li>
            <li><strong>Run the downstairs test.</strong> With a neighbour's help, or with a phone recording from the room below, walk for a few minutes at your usual pace, then slower, then in different shoes. Note what changes.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the incidental noises.</strong> Start, stop, change speed and fold it. If the beeps cannot be muted or the fold clunks, decide now whether you can live with them.</li>
            <li><strong>Use it normally for a week.</strong> Some noise appears only after the belt has bedded in, and some disappears. Listen for squeaks, ticks or a rhythmic slap, which usually mean the belt needs adjustment or lubricant.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If it is still too loud after you have done all that, return it within the window and
            step up to a heavier machine rather than layering more padding underneath. Stacking soft
            materials under a pad tends to make it rock, which brings its own noise and is not
            stable underfoot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you want to jog</strong>, a walking pad is the wrong machine for an apartment,
            quiet or not. Jogging multiplies impact noise, and most pads are not built for it. Our
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            explains the limits, and a full treadmill on a dense mat is the better route if running
            matters to you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you live above a light timber floor and walk for hours every day</strong>,
            expect some noise to reach the flat below with any machine. A heavier office treadmill
            and a dense mat reduce it; an honest conversation with the neighbour, and an agreement on
            hours, may do more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the noise you mind is your own</strong> — the hum while you are on calls —
            any of the folding pads here at a gentle desk pace is likely to be fine, and you need not
            pay for a heavy office machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you would rather not walk at your desk at all</strong>, a full desk set-up
            may suit you better than a loose pad. Our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill desks</a>
            compares the complete options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the quietest walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no reliable answer, because walking pad makers do not measure noise the same way and listing decibel figures cannot be compared. For neighbours below, heavier non-folding machines such as the LifeSpan TR1200Pro Glow-Up or the iMovR Unsit are the safer bet. Among folding pads, independent reviewers found the WalkingPad A1 Pro fairly quiet at desk speeds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can downstairs neighbours hear a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes, though usually as a rhythmic thud from your footsteps rather than motor noise. Impact travels through the floor structure far more easily than motor hum does. A dense rubber mat, a heavier machine, a slower pace, softer steps and keeping the pad away from shared walls all reduce it. On a timber floor, some noise usually still gets through.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are walking pad decibel ratings accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Treat them as the seller's claim. Listings rarely say how far from the machine, at what speed or with what load the figure was taken, and two listings from the same brand can quote very different numbers for similar pads. Even a careful airborne measurement says nothing about impact noise in the room below, which is the usual complaint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a mat make a walking pad quieter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dense rubber mat reduces some of the impact noise that reaches the floor and stops rattles, and it protects the floor. A thin vinyl mat mainly protects the floor. Neither does much for motor hum in your own room. Soft foam is a poor choice because it lets the pad rock. A mat helps; it does not make a pad silent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a walking pad quieter than a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In the room, usually yes, because walking pads run slower with smaller motors. Through the floor, not necessarily: a light pad has little mass and cushioning to absorb your steps before they reach the floor, so a heavier treadmill at the same walking pace can be the better neighbour. What matters most is pace, mass and what sits underneath.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What time is it OK to use a walking pad in an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check your lease or building rules first, because some set quiet hours. Otherwise, impact noise is most intrusive early in the morning and late at night when the flat below is quiet. Daytime use is less likely to cause complaints. If you plan regular early walks, a short conversation with the neighbour below is worth having.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Quiet Walking Pad</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these leads to a pad that is louder downstairs than the listing promised.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by the decibel figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Listing dB figures are the seller's claims, measured in unknown conditions, and they describe motor hum rather than the thud below. Two listings from the same brand quoted 40 and 65 dB for similar pads on the day we checked. Choose by mass, deck, speed range and independent descriptions of noise, and treat any number as marketing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a light pad straight on a timber floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 50 lb folding pad on a suspended timber floor passes most of each footstep into the structure. Put a dense rubber mat underneath, keep the pad away from shared walls and away from the middle of a long span, and consider a heavier non-folding machine if you walk for hours above someone's bedroom.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Walking too fast with a heavy heel strike</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Impact noise rises sharply with pace and with how hard your heels land. A brisk 4 mph walk in hard-soled shoes is the loudest way to use any pad. Slow down to a pace you can hold with a soft, rolling step, wear cushioned shoes, and save faster walking for times when the flat below is empty.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring beeps, hinges and maintenance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many complaints are about incidental noise: beeps on every speed change that cannot be muted, a hinge that clunks on unfolding, or a dry belt that squeaks. Check whether the beeps can be silenced before buying, unfold a folding pad gently, and lubricate and centre the belt on the maker's schedule so the pad stays quiet as it ages.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

